ass: blur in scratch memory, straight from the cached bitmap

A gaussian blur allocated its stripe buffers (eight bytes per pixel of the
padded bitmap) and freed them again for every bitmap, and before it the
glyphs of a run were added up in a bitmap that was allocated zeroed, read
once by the blur and freed; a single glyph or drawing was copied for the
same purpose. Each of these is large enough for the allocator to map and
unmap it: on Android, jemalloc purges at once, so every blur paid for an
mmap, a page fault per page and a munmap, three times over. In the profile
of a typeset episode on a Fire TV Stick 4K Max malloc and free were a
tenth of all rendering time.

Each render thread now keeps the stripe buffers and the blur's input
between bitmaps, and a single cached bitmap is blurred as it is, without
the copy. The result is allocated as before, since it is what the cache
keeps. Without a gaussian blur nothing changes.

What a thread keeps is bounded twice: a buffer over 8 MB is released right
after use, and every 256 frames each thread releases what it has not used
since the sweep before, so a scene of large blurs costs its memory for ten
to twenty seconds after it, not for as long as the renderer lives.

